// Title: Potential Persistence Via New AMSI Providers - Registry
// ID: 33efc23c-6ea2-4503-8cfe-bdf82ce8f705
// Status: test
// Level: medium
// Author: Nasreddine Bencherchali (Nextron Systems)
// Date: 2022-07-21
// Tags: attack.persistence
// Description: Detects when an attacker adds a new AMSI provider via the Windows Registry to bypass AMSI (Antimalware Scan Interface) protections.
// Attackers may add custom AMSI providers to persist on the system and evade detection by security software that relies on AMSI for scanning scripts and other content.
// This technique is often used in conjunction with fileless malware and script-based attacks to maintain persistence while avoiding detection.
// Converted by: Sigma Universal SIEM/EDR CLI

source = logs-* | where (((registry.path like "%\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\AMSI\\Providers\\%" OR registry.path like "%\\SOFTWARE\\WOW6432Node\\Microsoft\\AMSI\\Providers\\%")) AND NOT ((((process.executable = "C:\\Program Files\\Avast Software\\Avast\\RegSvr.exe" OR process.executable = "C:\\Program Files\\Avast Software\\Avast\\x86\\RegSvr.exe") AND registry.path like "%\\{FB904E4E-D2C7-4C8D-8492-B620BB9896B1}%") OR ((process.executable = "C:\\Program Files\\AVG\\Antivirus\\RegSvr.exe" OR process.executable = "C:\\Program Files\\AVG\\Antivirus\\x86\\RegSvr.exe") AND registry.path like "%\\{FB904E4E-D2C7-4C8D-8492-B620BB9896B1}%") OR (process.executable = "C:\\Program Files\\Avira\\Endpoint Protection SDK\\endpointprotection.exe" AND registry.path like "%\\{00000001-3DCC-4B48-A82E-E2071FE58E05}%"))))
